{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-b8ce2176-93fa-42cf-80ef-d4568788efc0","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-bf873fe1f7cc6ee1","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-8003-dvw25u6y/artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-8003-E002.md","content":"# MaaS Helm chart packaging for xKS deployment\n\n## Description\n\nPackages the MaaS components (maas-api, maas-controller, and the payload-processing Envoy external processor) as Helm charts installable on non-OpenShift Kubernetes (xKS: CKS, AKS, EKS, GKE), replacing the OLM-based operator installation used on RHOAI. The charts expose configuration for everything that is OpenShift-specific today: gateway class, identity provider, TLS certificate source, and database connection. This is Tier 1 work — it delivers value regardless of how the RHCL/Kuadrant support question resolves.\n\n## Scope\n\n- Helm charts for maas-api, maas-controller, and the payload-processing ext_proc component, with configurable values for:\n  - GatewayClass name (Istio for CKS; other implementations for other xKS platforms) instead of `openshift-default`.\n  - External OIDC issuer configuration (issuer URL, client ID, audience, group claim path).\n  - TLS certificate source via cert-manager, replacing OpenShift service-CA auto-generated certificates.\n  - PostgreSQL connection, supporting either a dedicated database or one shared with other RHAII components.\n- Removal of OpenShift-specific packaging assumptions from the xKS installation path: no OLM dependency; CRDs and validating admission webhooks installed via the chart on non-OLM clusters.\n- Helm chart build pipeline in the repository's existing Konflux-based build infrastructure.\n- Composability with the wider RHAII xKS installation tooling (installable standalone and as part of the RHAII xKS deployment).\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- `helm install` of the chart on a non-OpenShift Kubernetes cluster results in running and healthy maas-api and maas-controller pods, measured by pod readiness checks passing.\n- The MaaS HTTPRoutes attach to a configurable GatewayClass (not `openshift-default`), verified with an Istio GatewayClass on a CKS-compatible environment.\n- Chart values allow configuring the OIDC issuer (URL, client ID, audience), the group claim path, the TLS certificate source via cert-manager, and the PostgreSQL connection (shared or dedicated) without modifying the chart.\n- TLS certificates issued via cert-manager renew before expiration without manual intervention.\n- The Helm chart build pipeline produces publishable chart artifacts (build pipeline green).\n- The xKS installation path contains no OLM dependency (Helm-only installation per the strategy's out-of-scope statement on operator integration).\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- Enables HLR-1 (P0): MaaS successfully deployed on CKS.\n- Enables HLR-2 (P0): MaaS successfully deployed on AKS.\n- Supports HLR-4 (P0): provides the configuration surface for external OIDC issuer settings.\n\n## Notes\n\n- The Helm chart build pipeline prerequisite is listed in the strategy as \"Pending human review\"; it is folded into this epic as an acceptance criterion rather than a separate onboarding epic, because the repository is already Konflux-onboarded for container images (per architecture context).\n- The shared-vs-dedicated PostgreSQL decision (open question owned by xKS Platform) is a deployment choice; the chart supports both, so it does not gate this epic.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":82488,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b3d12adea12401d9","agent_id":"a585326c42577ebb9","session_id":"03f22d79-03f4-4cd2-8583-e4fd3448986e","uuid":"44189dcb-5625-42be-a063-b37be1d2534e","timestamp":"2026-10-22T04:58:28.441Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"D