{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-0f16fe6d-3b6d-42ce-a63c-b5ae054a60cc","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-a4aeb53b7355ba31","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-8003-dvw25u6y/artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-8003-E011.md","content":"# User identity flow documentation for MaaS on xKS\n\n## Description\n\nDocuments the user identity flow for MaaS on xKS deployments: how customers integrate their own OIDC-compatible identity provider, how external identities map to MaaS subscriptions and tiers, and what users must provide and configure. This is the user-management deliverable of the strategy (\"User authentication strategy defined and documented\", \"User identity flow documented for xKS deployments\"). It is a docs-authoring epic and runs last: the content describes what was built and validated.\n\n## Scope\n\nContent outline:\n\n- User identity flow for xKS deployments (end-to-end narrative: user → OIDC provider → gateway authentication → MaaS identity → subscription/tier).\n- Customer identity-provider requirements: standards-compliant OIDC providers (Keycloak, Entra ID, Google Identity Platform, Cognito, and similar), and what users must provide (their own OIDC-compatible identity provider).\n- Configuration guidance: OIDC issuer URL, client ID, audience, and group claim path — including provider-specific group claim formats (e.g., Entra ID `groups`, Cognito `cognito:groups`).\n- Identity-to-subscription/tier mapping: how group claims select subscription tiers and rate limits.\n- API key lifecycle under the xKS identity model (creation, use, revocation; OIDC-independent validation).\n- Known identity-management limitations on xKS versus RHOAI MaaS GA, cross-referenced from the validation findings.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- A published document describes the user identity flow for xKS deployments, covering OIDC provider requirements, configuration, group-claim-to-subscription/tier mapping, and the API key lifecycle.\n- The document is technically reviewed against the implemented and validated authentication behavior (review sign-off recorded).\n- The documented flow is consistent with the validated behavior on CKS and AKS and with the known-limitations document (no contradictions).\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- HLR-12 (P2): User identity flow documented for xKS deployments.\n- Also delivers the strategy acceptance criteria \"User authentication strategy defined and documented\" and \"User identity flow documented for xKS deployments\".\n\n## Notes\n\n- Priority derivation (docs-authoring exception): derived from the strategy frontmatter priority (`Blocker`), which sits above Critical and therefore maps to the P0 bucket — not from the P2 HLR it covers, and it does not propagate priority upstream to the implementation epics.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":89170,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b3d12adea12401d9","agent_id":"a585326c42577ebb9","session_id":"03f22d79-03f4-4cd2-8583-e4fd3448986e","uuid":"c332b076-da17-48ca-8325-dae2919499b2","timestamp":"2026-10-22T04:59:58.304Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Decompose RHAISTRAT-8003"}
