{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-96acfa66cca6e55b","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tepic_id: RHAISTRAT-8003-E007\n3\ttitle: MaaS deployment validation on CKS\n4\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8003\n5\tcomponent: Model as a Service\n6\tteam: MaaS\n7\ttype: Implementation\n8\tpriority: P0\n9\tdependencies:\n10\t- RHAISTRAT-8003-E001\n11\t- RHAISTRAT-8003-E002\n12\t- RHAISTRAT-8003-E003\n13\t- RHAISTRAT-8003-E004\n14\tgated_by: RHAISTRAT-8003-E001\n15\tgate_failure_impact:\n16\t  action: rewrite\n17\t  fallback_approach: 'If the Envoy ext_authz contingency is selected, validation scope\n18\t    changes: no Kuadrant to install; enforcement behavior validated against the Envoy-native\n19\t    integration instead'\n20\tai_signals:\n21\t  change_specificity: 1\n22\t  pattern_precedent: 1\n23\t  adapter_pattern: 0\n24\t  existing_foundation: 1\n25\t  open_questions: 0\n26\t  external_dependency: -1\n27\t  human_process_gates: 0\n28\t  repo_access: 1\n29\t  architecture_claims: 1\n30\timplementation_type: null\n31\tinvestigation_signals: null\n32\tai_implementability: High\n33\tai_implementability_score: 4\n34\tjira_key: null\n35\tbranch: null\n36\t---\n37\t# MaaS deployment validation on CKS\n38\t\n39\t## Description\n40\t\n41\tDeploys MaaS on CKS (CoreWeave Kubernetes Service — the priority platform per the strategy) via the Helm charts with external OIDC configuration, and validates core MaaS functionality end to end: authentication, API key lifecycle, subscription/tier management, and token rate limiting. Documents what works, what breaks, and the engineering delta versus RHOAI MaaS GA, including the consolidated known-limitations document. This epic is gated on the RHCL/Kuadrant xKS support investigation: the deployment validation requires the Kuadrant deployment mechanism to be selected, and its scope changes if the Envoy ext_authz contingency is selected.\n42\t\n43\t## Scope\n44\t\n45\t- Deploy MaaS on a CKS cluster with RHCL/Kuadrant (deployed per the selected deployment mechanism) and the Istio GatewayClass, using the Helm charts with OIDC configuration.\n46\t- Validate core functionality end to end:\n47\t  - Pod health/readiness of maas-api and maas-controller.\n48\t  - OIDC user authentication, API key creation, and inference using the generated API key.\n49\t  - Token rate limiting: HTTP 429 on exceeded quota (Limitador enforcement).\n50\t  - Tier-based rate limiting via configured group claims.\n51\t- Validate failure modes:\n52\t  - Limitador pod termination (enforcement unavailable).\n53\t  - cert-manager TLS certificate rotation without interruption.\n54\t  - PostgreSQL unavailability (API key creation/validation behavior while OIDC authentication continues).\n55\t- Document the engineering delta discovered on CKS (code changes, configuration changes, Helm chart fixes).\n56\t- Produce the consolidated known-limitations document (MaaS on xKS versus RHOAI MaaS GA).\n57\t\n58\t## Acceptance Criteria\n59\t\n60\t- Given a CKS cluster with RHCL/Kuadrant and Gateway API (Istio) installed, when MaaS is deployed via Helm chart with OIDC configuration, then the maas-api and maas-controller pods are running and healthy, measured by pod readiness checks passing.\n61\t- Given the deployed MaaS instance with OIDC configured, when a user authenticates with a valid OIDC JWT token and creates an API key, then the API key is persisted in PostgreSQL and can be used for model inference, measured by a successful inference request using the generated API key.\n62\t- Given a MaaSSubscription with token rate limits configured, when a user exceeds the rate limit, then HTTP 429 is returned, measured by monitoring response status codes.\n63\t- Given a deployed MaaS deployment with the Limitador pod terminated, when a user makes an inference request, then the request is denied with HTTP 503, measured by response status and Envoy logs showing upstream connection failure.\n64\t- Given cert-manager-issued TLS certificates, when the certificates are rotated (renewal before expiration), then active inference requests continue without interruption and new requests succeed with the new certificate, measured by zero connection errors during the rotation window and successful TLS handshake validation post-rotation.\n65\t- Given the PostgreSQL database becomes unavailable, then API key creation requests fail with HTTP 503 and existing API key validation follows the documented caching behavior, while OIDC token-based authentication continues to succeed, measured by response status and error logs.\n66\t- A document exists listing all known limitations of MaaS on xKS versus RHOAI MaaS GA.\n67\t- The engineering delta (what worked, what broke, what was changed) is documented.\n68\t\n69\t## HLR Traceability\n70\t\n71\t- HLR-1 (P0): MaaS successfully deployed on CKS (priority platform).\n72\t- HLR-3 (P0): Token rate limiting functional on xKS.\n73\t- HLR-11 (P1): Known limitations documented (consolidated document).\n74\t- End-to-end confirmation of HLR-4, HLR-5, HLR-6, and HLR-9 on CKS (primary coverage in the OIDC integration and group claim mapping epics).\n75\t\n76\t## Notes\n77\t\n78\t- Schedule risk carried from the strategy: MaaS 3.4 GA stable build availability is unconfirmed; mitigations (RC build, parallel validation start, RHOAI 3.3 fallback demo) are contingency actions, not acceptance criteria.\n79\t- This epic consumes the Helm charts, the external OIDC authentication integration, and the configurable group claim path; CKS-first sequencing per the strategy, with findings feeding the AKS validation.\n80\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a608d0b6c1ea84d3","agent_id":"a8dbaf530c1362791","session_id":"03f22d79-03f4-4cd2-8583-e4fd3448986e","uuid":"97b43b5b-bb7e-469a-ae7e-69400286623f","timestamp":"2026-10-22T05:04:36.384Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-96acfa66cca6e55b","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Review RHAISTRAT-8003 decomposition"}
