{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-8a8f6834c7470d2c","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tepic_id: RHAISTRAT-8003-E008\n3\ttitle: MaaS deployment validation on AKS\n4\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8003\n5\tcomponent: Model as a Service\n6\tteam: MaaS\n7\ttype: Implementation\n8\tpriority: P0\n9\tdependencies:\n10\t- RHAISTRAT-8003-E007\n11\tai_signals:\n12\t  change_specificity: 1\n13\t  pattern_precedent: 1\n14\t  adapter_pattern: 0\n15\t  existing_foundation: 1\n16\t  open_questions: -1\n17\t  external_dependency: -1\n18\t  human_process_gates: 0\n19\t  repo_access: 1\n20\t  architecture_claims: 0\n21\timplementation_type: null\n22\tinvestigation_signals: null\n23\tai_implementability: Medium\n24\tai_implementability_score: 2\n25\tjira_key: null\n26\tbranch: null\n27\tgated_by: null\n28\tgate_failure_impact: null\n29\t---\n30\t# MaaS deployment validation on AKS\n31\t\n32\t## Description\n33\t\n34\tExtends the validated MaaS xKS deployment to AKS (Azure Kubernetes Service), satisfying the RFE acceptance criterion requiring deployment on \"AKS AND CKS\". Builds on the CKS validation: the Helm charts fixed and the engineering delta documented during CKS validation are the starting point. Documents AKS-specific limitations and configuration differences.\n35\t\n36\t## Scope\n37\t\n38\t- Deploy MaaS on an AKS cluster via the Helm charts with external OIDC configuration, using a Gateway API implementation available on AKS (per the strategy's noted options for non-CKS platforms: `envoy-gateway` multi-platform or `azure-application-gateway`).\n39\t- Validate the same core functionality as the CKS validation: pod health, OIDC authentication, API key creation and inference, subscription management, and token rate limiting.\n40\t- Document AKS-specific known limitations and configuration differences (added to the consolidated known-limitations document).\n41\t\n42\t## Acceptance Criteria\n43\t\n44\t- Given an AKS cluster with RHCL/Kuadrant and a Gateway API implementation installed, when MaaS is deployed via Helm chart with OIDC configuration, then the maas-api and maas-controller pods are running and healthy, measured by pod readiness checks passing.\n45\t- Core functionality is validated on AKS: OIDC authentication, API key-based inference, subscription management, and token rate limiting (HTTP 429 on exceeded quota), measured by the same checks used for the CKS validation.\n46\t- AKS-specific known limitations and configuration differences are documented and added to the known-limitations document.\n47\t\n48\t## HLR Traceability\n49\t\n50\t- HLR-2 (P0): MaaS successfully deployed on AKS (RFE acceptance criterion \"AKS AND CKS\").\n51\t- HLR-11 (P1): Known limitations documented (AKS-specific findings).\n52\t\n53\t## Notes\n54\t\n55\t- The choice of Gateway API implementation on AKS (`envoy-gateway` vs. `azure-application-gateway`) is an implementation decision made when this epic starts; both are within scope.\n56\t- Runs after the CKS validation per the strategy's explicit CKS-first sequencing (priority platform); the charts and documented engineering delta from CKS are prerequisites.\n57\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a608d0b6c1ea84d3","agent_id":"a8dbaf530c1362791","session_id":"03f22d79-03f4-4cd2-8583-e4fd3448986e","uuid":"df4b479f-e11e-498b-b782-6c5c5f32587f","timestamp":"2026-10-22T05:04:37.076Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-8a8f6834c7470d2c","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Review RHAISTRAT-8003 decomposition"}
