{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b29a98b1e64ba3e7","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tepic_id: RHAISTRAT-8003-E011\n3\ttitle: User identity flow documentation for MaaS on xKS\n4\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8003\n5\tcomponent: Documentation\n6\tteam: MaaS\n7\ttype: Implementation\n8\timplementation_type: docs-authoring\n9\tpriority: P0\n10\tdependencies:\n11\t- RHAISTRAT-8003-E002\n12\t- RHAISTRAT-8003-E003\n13\t- RHAISTRAT-8003-E004\n14\t- RHAISTRAT-8003-E005\n15\t- RHAISTRAT-8003-E006\n16\t- RHAISTRAT-8003-E007\n17\t- RHAISTRAT-8003-E008\n18\t- RHAISTRAT-8003-E009\n19\t- RHAISTRAT-8003-E010\n20\tai_signals:\n21\t  change_specificity: 1\n22\t  pattern_precedent: 0\n23\t  adapter_pattern: 0\n24\t  existing_foundation: 0\n25\t  open_questions: 1\n26\t  external_dependency: 0\n27\t  human_process_gates: -1\n28\t  repo_access: 1\n29\t  architecture_claims: 0\n30\tinvestigation_signals: null\n31\tai_implementability: Medium\n32\tai_implementability_score: 2\n33\tjira_key: null\n34\tbranch: null\n35\tgated_by: null\n36\tgate_failure_impact: null\n37\t---\n38\t# User identity flow documentation for MaaS on xKS\n39\t\n40\t## Description\n41\t\n42\tDocuments the user identity flow for MaaS on xKS deployments: how customers integrate their own OIDC-compatible identity provider, how external identities map to MaaS subscriptions and tiers, and what users must provide and configure. This is the user-management deliverable of the strategy (\"User authentication strategy defined and documented\", \"User identity flow documented for xKS deployments\"). It is a docs-authoring epic and runs last: the content describes what was built and validated.\n43\t\n44\t## Scope\n45\t\n46\tContent outline:\n47\t\n48\t- User identity flow for xKS deployments (end-to-end narrative: user → OIDC provider → gateway authentication → MaaS identity → subscription/tier).\n49\t- Customer identity-provider requirements: standards-compliant OIDC providers (Keycloak, Entra ID, Google Identity Platform, Cognito, and similar), and what users must provide (their own OIDC-compatible identity provider).\n50\t- Configuration guidance: OIDC issuer URL, client ID, audience, and group claim path — including provider-specific group claim formats (e.g., Entra ID `groups`, Cognito `cognito:groups`).\n51\t- Identity-to-subscription/tier mapping: how group claims select subscription tiers and rate limits.\n52\t- API key lifecycle under the xKS identity model (creation, use, revocation; OIDC-independent validation).\n53\t- Known identity-management limitations on xKS versus RHOAI MaaS GA, cross-referenced from the validation findings.\n54\t\n55\t## Acceptance Criteria\n56\t\n57\t- A published document describes the user identity flow for xKS deployments, covering OIDC provider requirements, configuration, group-claim-to-subscription/tier mapping, and the API key lifecycle.\n58\t- The document is technically reviewed against the implemented and validated authentication behavior (review sign-off recorded).\n59\t- The documented flow is consistent with the validated behavior on CKS and AKS and with the known-limitations document (no contradictions).\n60\t\n61\t## HLR Traceability\n62\t\n63\t- HLR-12 (P2): User identity flow documented for xKS deployments.\n64\t- Also delivers the strategy acceptance criteria \"User authentication strategy defined and documented\" and \"User identity flow documented for xKS deployments\".\n65\t\n66\t## Notes\n67\t\n68\t- Priority derivation (docs-authoring exception): derived from the strategy frontmatter priority (`Blocker`), which sits above Critical and therefore maps to the P0 bucket — not from the P2 HLR it covers, and it does not propagate priority upstream to the implementation epics.\n69\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a608d0b6c1ea84d3","agent_id":"a8dbaf530c1362791","session_id":"03f22d79-03f4-4cd2-8583-e4fd3448986e","uuid":"d2392ead-1f24-44fe-a797-25be64878abe","timestamp":"2026-10-