{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9b8f872c7985a633","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tstrat_id: RHAISTRAT-8003\n3\tscore: 13\n4\tpass: true\n5\trecommendation: accept\n6\tissues:\n7\t- severity: minor\n8\t  criterion: AI Implementability Scoring\n9\t  description: 'E009 scores the CKS Grafana Loki assumption open_questions: 1 / external_dependency:\n10\t    0 while sibling E006 scores the parallel CKS Prometheus assumption open_questions:\n11\t    -1 / external_dependency: -1 - the same strategy-flagged needs-validation infrastructure\n12\t    class is scored oppositely across epics; align E009 with E006 or justify the difference'\n13\t- severity: minor\n14\t  criterion: AI Implementability Scoring\n15\t  description: 'E003 open_questions: -1 cites the OIDC-outage caching strategy and\n16\t    the AuthPolicy open question while the epic body calls the caching question an\n17\t    implementation decision, not a scope unknown - body text and signal rationale\n18\t    pull in different directions'\n19\t- severity: minor\n20\t  criterion: Epic Boundaries\n21\t  description: E001 (RHCL / OpenShift team) bundles the CKS Prometheus/Loki accessibility\n22\t    confirmation that the strategy open-questions table assigns to xKS Platform /\n23\t    CoreWeave, and E006 re-confirms the Prometheus part as an AC - move the infrastructure\n24\t    confirmation to the xKS Platform side or note the dual coverage\n25\t- severity: minor\n26\t  criterion: Acceptance Criteria Quality\n27\t  description: E004 adds the Tenant CRD groupClaimPath field without an explicit backwards-compatibility\n28\t    AC (default preserves auth.identity.groups behavior), unlike the additive ACs\n29\t    stated on E003/E005 for the same strategy NFR\n30\t- severity: minor\n31\t  criterion: Completeness\n32\t  description: Under branch C there is no Kuadrant, so E009 delegation of gateway-side\n33\t    structured logging to the selected Kuadrant deployment mechanism has no referent\n34\t    and BRANCH-C-E012 has no enforcement-path logging scope - gateway-side auth/rate-limit\n35\t    decision logs are unowned if the contingency activates\n36\terror: null\n37\t---\n38\t## Review Summary\n39\t\n40\tScore: 13/14 — pass\n41\tRecommendation: accept\n42\t\n43\tFull decomposition (triage absent, 11 main-plan epics + 3 conditional branch epics) of an M-sized, multi-team strategy. Contract validator: OK (body sections, canonical components, Rule 11 docs-last coverage, and priority inheritance all pass). The DAG diagram and epic frontmatter `dependencies` match edge-for-edge; all 13 HLRs are traceable; the single Investigation (E001) genuinely gates three bounded branch outcomes, and no main-plan epic depends on a branch node. Remaining findings are minor.\n44\t\n45\t## Criterion Details\n46\t\n47\t### 1. HLR Coverage (2/2)\n48\tAll 13 HLRs map to at least one epic; the traceability matrix in the summary is complete and matches each epic's HLR Traceability section. All P0 HLRs covered (HLR-1/2 by E007/E008; HLR-3 by E007 + branch epics; HLR-4/5 by E003; HLR-6 by E004; HLR-7 by E001 + branches; HLR-8 by E005+E006). P1 HLR-9 is correctly consolidated with HLR-6 into E004 — same controller surface, and the consolidation note explains why a split would rebuild the same surface; this matches the boundary guidance. P2 HLR-12 (E011) and HLR-13 (E010) are implemented, not deferred. Priority inheritance is clean: E009 (P1) and E010 (P2) block only E011, a docs-authoring epic exempt from inheritance; no lower-priority epic blocks P0 implementation work. No priority collapse with deferrable features (metrics/logging/tracing correctly separated into E005/E009/E010).\n49\t\n50\t### 2. DAG Coherence (2/2)\n51\tNo cycles. Every edge in the summary diagram has a matching `dependencies` entry in the target epic's frontmatter and vice versa (verified edge-by-edge for all 17 main-plan edges); arrows point dependency → dependent per convention. Blocking edges are justified: E002/E003/E004 → E007 (artifact consumers of charts/auth/claim-path), E005 → E006 and E007 → E006/E009/E010 (ACs measured against a deployed instance with live data), E007 → E008 (strategy's explicit CKS-first sequencing), E002…E010 → E011 (Rule 11 docs-last). Tier-1 work (E002/E003/E004/E005) is correctly parallel with no serializing edges. No konflux-onboarding epic exists, so rule 7/8/10 parallelism constraints are moot; the Helm build pipeline is correctly a Rule 25 AC on E002, never a DAG edge. Branch files all set both `branch` and `gated_by` (E001), and no main-plan epic lists a branch epic in `dependencies` — main-plan epics needing the outcome (E003, E007) depend on the Investigation itself. Critical path length 5 (E001→E003→E007→E008→E011) is within the M-with-new-component heuristic (4-5). No undiscovered data dependencies: every epic consuming charts, metrics, or a deployed instance has an edge to its producer.\n52\t\n53\t### 3. Epic Boundaries (2/2)\n54\tEach epic carries a single canonical component (Model as a Service, Inference Gateway, Monitoring, Documentation — all in `.context/rhai-components.txt`) and a single team, matching the strategy's impacted-teams table. No oversized epic: E007 bundles deploy + validation + failure modes + findings docs, but it is one validation campaign on one platform, within sprint scale. No scope duplication: E005 (instrumentation) vs E006 (dashboards/integration) and E007 vs E008 (CKS vs AKS, separate HLRs and platform configs) are complementary, not overlapping surfaces. No work-product type mixing — the sole docs-authoring epic (E011) is separate from all implementation epics; E007's known-limitations document is a validation output, not content authoring. One minor nit: E001 (RHCL / OpenShift team) bundles the CKS Prometheus/Loki accessibility confirmation, which the strategy's open-questions table assigns to xKS Platform / CoreWeave, and E006's AC re-confirms the Prometheus part — a boundary edge case, not a defect (the question gets answered either way).\n55\t\n56\t### 4. Type Correctness (2/2)\n57\tE001 is a genuine Investigation: its outcome selects among three bounded deployment mechanisms, changes the scope of E003 and E007 (both `gate_failure_impact: rewrite` with populated fallback approaches), and instantiates one of three branch epics. All Implementation epics produce concrete artifacts (charts, config, code instrumentation, dashboards, validated deployments, documents). Every epic listing E001 in `dependencies` (E003, E007, and all three branch epics) sets `gated_by: E001`, and every `gated_by` target appears in that epic's direct `dependencies`. Every non-null `gate_failure_impact` has both `action` and `fallback_approach` populated, and each describes a real scope/existence change, not a scheduling dependency. No `gated_by` without `dependencies` linkage.\n58\t\n59\t### 5. AI Implementability Scoring (1/2)\n60\tType-appropriate rubrics used throughout: E001 carries `investigation_signals` (5-signal), all Implementation epics including E011 and the branch epics carry `ai_signals` (9-signal); no cross-type contamination. Rationales are present for all 14 epics and cross-check well against architecture context — the Prometheus client/metrics-listener (port 9090), OTLP/gRPC trace export, and Dockerfile.konflux claims in the E005/E010/E002 rationales are all corroborated by `.context/architecture-context/architecture/rhoai-3.6-ea.1/models-as-a-service.md`. Two arguable values, both around the CKS infrastructure assumptions: (a) E009 scores the CKS Grafana Loki assumption `open_questions: 1` / `external_dependency: 0` (\"assumed CoreWeave-standard infrastructure, validated as an AC\") while sibling E006 scores the parallel CKS Prometheus assumption `open_questions: -1` / `external_dependency: -1` for the same class of strategy-flagged, needs-validation infrastructure — the same dependency class is scored oppositely across epics; (b) E003's `open_questions: -1` cites the OIDC-outage caching strategy and the AuthPolicy open question, while the epic body calls the caching question \"an implementation decision, not a scope unknown\" — the signal is the conservative reading, but body and rationale pull in different directions. Arithmetic/thresholds not checked (computed by pipeline).\n61\t\n62\t### 6. Acceptance Criteria Quality (2/2)\n63\tEvery epic has testable ACs, largely lifted verbatim from the strategy's Given/When/Then criteria. Rule-mandated ACs present where applicable: E003 and E005 carry explicit additive/backwards-compatibility ACs; E011 (docs-authoring) has the technical-review sign-off AC; E002 carries the build-pipeline-green AC (Rule 25) in place of an onboarding epic. Schedule risk (MaaS 3.4 build timing, Summit) is correctly kept in Notes/Health Warnings rather than filed as an AC — no non-testable external-schedule ACs found. No epic body references sibling epics by draft ID (checked all 14 bodies). One minor nit: E004 adds a Tenant CRD field (defaulting to `auth.identity.groups`) without an explicit backwards-compatibility AC, though the strategy NFR requires additive/configuration-driven changes and the default makes the change inherently additive; E003/E005 state this AC explicitly where E004 does not.\n64\t\n65\t### 7. Completeness (2/2)\n66\tStrategy scope is fully accounted for: user management strategy (E003/E004/E011), CKS+AKS validation with the AKS-vs-CKS scope contradiction surfaced as a health warning rather than silently resolved, all four failure-mode criteria (OIDC outage → E003; Limitador termination, cert rotation, PostgreSQL unavailability → E007), observability tiers P0/P1/P2 (E005/E006, E009, E010), the RHCL strategic dependency with all three bounded outcomes as branch epics, known limitations (E007/E008), and the identity-flow documentation (E011). Cross-epic consistency holds: every configurable value the charts expose (E002) has an owning implementation epic (E003 OIDC, E004 claim path), and all are consumed by E007. Strategy-level context is carried forward (risks in epic notes, health warnings for the AKS/CKS contradiction, tracing priority inconsistency, empty Staff Engineer Input, pending process gates). One minor gap on a conditional path: under branch C there is no Kuadrant, so E009's delegation of gateway-side structured logging to \"the selected Kuadrant deployment mechanism\" has no referent, and BRANCH-C-E012's scope includes no enforcement-path logging — HLR-10's named items all remain covered by E009's maas-api-side ACs, but gateway-side auth/rate-limit decision logs are unowned if the contingency activates.\n67\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-8a17c4912b5196bb","agent_id":"a95517014c36948fe","session_id":"03f22d79-03f4-4cd2-8583-e4fd3448986e","uuid":"7c663e98-4f40-4147-8428-df10913afc5f","timestamp":"2026-10-22T05:09:38.320Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-9b8f872c7985a633","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Revise RHAISTRAT-8003 decomposition"}
