{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-92a448ed1f461fd2","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tepic_id: RHAISTRAT-8003-E003\n3\ttitle: External OIDC authentication integration for MaaS on xKS\n4\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8003\n5\tcomponent: Model as a Service\n6\tteam: MaaS\n7\ttype: Implementation\n8\tpriority: P0\n9\tdependencies:\n10\t- RHAISTRAT-8003-E001\n11\tgated_by: RHAISTRAT-8003-E001\n12\tgate_failure_impact:\n13\t  action: rewrite\n14\t  fallback_approach: If the direct Envoy ext_authz contingency is selected, rework\n15\t    the authentication enforcement integration to the Envoy-native approach instead\n16\t    of Authorino AuthPolicy\n17\tai_signals:\n18\t  change_specificity: 1\n19\t  pattern_precedent: 1\n20\t  adapter_pattern: 1\n21\t  existing_foundation: 1\n22\t  open_questions: -1\n23\t  external_dependency: 0\n24\t  human_process_gates: 0\n25\t  repo_access: 1\n26\t  architecture_claims: 1\n27\timplementation_type: null\n28\tinvestigation_signals: null\n29\tai_implementability: High\n30\tai_implementability_score: 5\n31\tjira_key: null\n32\tbranch: null\n33\t---\n34\t# External OIDC authentication integration for MaaS on xKS\n35\t\n36\t## Description\n37\t\n38\tReplaces OpenShift's built-in OAuth/TokenReview-based user identity with external OIDC validation for MaaS on xKS. Managed Kubernetes services (CKS, AKS, EKS, GKE) do not expose OpenShift's identity infrastructure, so customers bring their own OIDC-compatible identity provider (Keycloak, Entra ID, Google Identity Platform, Cognito, or other standards-compliant providers) and Authorino validates JWT bearer tokens against it. API key authentication (the `sk-oai-*` flow) remains unchanged and OIDC-independent.\n39\t\n40\tThis work assumes Kuadrant/Authorino-based authentication enforcement. It is gated on the RHCL/Kuadrant xKS support investigation: if the direct Envoy ext_authz contingency is selected (neither official RHCL charts nor standalone Kuadrant viable), the enforcement integration is reworked to the Envoy-native approach.\n41\t\n42\t## Scope\n43\t\n44\t- Configure the MaaS AuthPolicy / Authorino authentication to use the external OIDC identity source (issuer URL, JWKS endpoint, audience) in place of KubernetesTokenReview against the OpenShift API server.\n45\t- Adapt OpenShift-specific identity assumptions in maas-api (internal token-validation paths that rely on Kubernetes TokenReview, SubjectAccessReview, and OpenShift identity mapping) for the xKS identity model.\n46\t- Preserve the API key validation flow via the maas-api callback path (`/internal/v1/api-keys/validate`) — it must remain OIDC-agnostic.\n47\t- Resolve and implement the OIDC validation behavior during identity-provider outages (caching/failure strategy — a strategy open question owned by Security / MaaS Engineering; its answer may affect the implementation approach, so it is resolved within this epic before the enforcement integration is finalized).\n48\t- Document the configuration requirements for standards-compliant OIDC providers.\n49\t\n50\t## Acceptance Criteria\n51\t\n52\t- Given a MaaS deployment with an external OIDC provider configured, when a user authenticates with a valid OIDC JWT token and creates an API key, then the API key is persisted in PostgreSQL and can be used for model inference, measured by a successful inference request using the generated API key.\n53\t- Given the OIDC provider becomes unavailable (network partition or IdP downtime), inference requests using valid API keys continue to succeed (API key validation is OIDC-independent), while OIDC-authenticated requests fail with HTTP 503 and a descriptive error message, measured by response status and error body.\n54\t- Given an invalid or expired OIDC JWT, the request is rejected with an appropriate error.\n55\t- The OIDC validation behavior during IdP outages (caching strategy) is documented and implemented.\n56\t- The change is additive/configuration-driven: RHOAI MaaS GA identity behavior is unchanged when OpenShift identity is used (backwards-compatibility NFR).\n57\t\n58\t## HLR Traceability\n59\t\n60\t- HLR-4 (P0): External OIDC authentication validated for user identity.\n61\t- HLR-5 (P0): API key authentication (`sk-oai-*` flow) functional on xKS (preserved and regression-verified by this epic).\n62\t\n63\t## Notes\n64\t\n65\t- Architecture context (models-as-a-service.md) confirms the AuthPolicy already supports API key extraction, Kubernetes TokenReview, and optional OIDC JWT validation — the work is reconfiguring and hardening the OIDC path as the primary identity source on xKS.\n66\t- The strategy's open question \"Can we leverage existing MaaS AuthPolicy with external OIDC, or are changes needed?\" is resolved by this epic's implementation; both outcomes are within its scope.\n67\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b289c4671c74508a","agent_id":"ab94395b3c5fb0286","session_id":"03f22d79-03f4-4cd2-8583-e4fd3448986e","uuid":"f7927911-5d16-4078-a66f-e16db0a4c030","timestamp":"2026-10-22T05:13:04.549Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-92a448ed1f461fd2","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Re-review RHAISTRAT-8003 decomposition"}
