{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9e08b53e7cc80e43","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tstrat_id: RHAISTRAT-8003\n3\tscore: 11\n4\tpass: false\n5\trecommendation: revise\n6\tissues:\n7\t- severity: critical\n8\t  criterion: DAG Coherence\n9\t  description: BRANCH-A/B-E012 (and BRANCH-C-E012 equivalently) have the acceptance\n10\t    criterion \"A MaaSSubscription with token rate limits configured returns HTTP 429\n11\t    (TokenRateLimitPolicy enforcement functional on CKS)\", which requires the MaaS\n12\t    control plane (MaaSSubscription CRD + maas-controller, which creates the TokenRateLimitPolicy\n13\t    per architecture context) delivered by E002 Helm charts — but the branch epics\n14\t    list only E001 in dependencies. Missing content-discoverable artifact dependency\n15\t    E002 -> branch epics; a branch activated before E002 lands cannot satisfy its\n16\t    own AC. Add E002 to the branch epics dependencies (branch-to-main-plan edges are\n17\t    permitted) or rescope the branch 429 AC to a directly applied TokenRateLimitPolicy.\n18\t- severity: minor\n19\t  criterion: Epic Boundaries\n20\t  description: E001 (RHCL / OpenShift team) bundles the CKS Prometheus/Loki accessibility\n21\t    confirmation that the strategy assigns to xKS Platform / CoreWeave; coordinated\n22\t    and dual-covered by E006/E009 ACs, but cross-team scope sits in one epic.\n23\t- severity: minor\n24\t  criterion: Epic Boundaries\n25\t  description: The HTTP 429 rate-limiting validation AC is duplicated between E007\n26\t    and BRANCH-A/B-E012, blurring the boundary between the deployment-mechanism branch\n27\t    and the CKS validation epic (redundant validation, not component rebuilding).\n28\t- severity: minor\n29\t  criterion: AI Implementability Scoring\n30\t  description: E003 architecture_claims=1 rationale claims the Tenant CRD OIDC fields\n31\t    (oidcIssuerURL/oidcClientID/oidcAudience) and auth-policy.yaml are corroborated\n32\t    by the models-as-a-service architecture context; the context file documents neither\n33\t    — corroboration overstated, value arguably 0.\n34\t- severity: minor\n35\t  criterion: AI Implementability Scoring\n36\t  description: E004 pattern_precedent=1 and architecture_claims=1 rationales claim\n37\t    architecture-context corroboration for the CEL predicates over auth.identity.groups\n38\t    in maassubscription_controller.go; the context file contains no CEL/group-claim\n39\t    mechanism or maassubscription_controller.go reference.\n40\t- severity: minor\n41\t  criterion: Acceptance Criteria Quality\n42\t  description: E002 scope includes composability with the RHAII xKS installation tooling\n43\t    but no AC tests it, while the summary health warning 8 states it is captured as\n44\t    an AC — summary/epic mismatch.\n45\t- severity: minor\n46\t  criterion: Completeness\n47\t  description: The strategy PostgreSQL-unavailability mitigation (in-memory LRU cache,\n48\t    TTL 5 min, for validated API key hashes) has no explicitly assigned epic scope;\n49\t    E007 AC defers to \"the documented caching behavior\" without an owning epic.\n50\terror: null\n51\t---\n52\t## Review Summary\n53\t\n54\tScore: 11/14 — fail\n55\tRecommendation: revise\n56\t\n57\tContract validator: OK (epic body sections, canonical component names, Rule 11 docs-last coverage, and priority inheritance all pass).\n58\t\n59\tOne critical DAG issue forces failure: the conditional branch epics' rate-limiting validation ACs consume MaaS artifacts (MaaSSubscription CRD + maas-controller, delivered by E002's Helm charts) but the branch epics list only E001 in `dependencies` — a content-discoverable artifact dependency with no edge. All other criteria score 2 or 1; the decomposition is otherwise strong (complete HLR traceability, diagram/frontmatter edge-for-edge consistency, well-formed gates and branch metadata, justified signal values with two over-corroborated rationales).\n60\t\n61\t## Criterion Details\n62\t\n63\t### 1. HLR Coverage (2/2)\n64\tAll 13 HLRs map to at least one epic; the traceability matrix is present and accurate. Every P0 HLR (HLR-1 through HLR-8) is covered by P0 epics; P1 HLR-10/11 and P2 HLR-12/13 are covered by same-priority epics (E009 P1, E010 P2, E011 docs-authoring P0 per the priority exception — strategy priority Blocker maps above Critical to P0). Priority inheritance verified: the only lower-priority epics (E009 P1, E010 P2) block only the docs-authoring epic, which is exempt; no lower-priority epic blocks P0 work. The HLR-6 (P0) + HLR-9 (P1) consolidation in E004 is justified, not priority collapse: HLR-9 is the functional expression of HLR-6's configuration capability on the same controller surface (the P1 cannot be deferred independently without stripping the P0 of purpose). Branch coverage of HLR-3/7/10 across all three outcomes is correct.\n65\t\n66\t### 2. DAG Coherence (0/2)\n67\tVerified: no circular dependencies; every diagram edge matches the target epic's frontmatter `dependencies` and vice versa (edge-by-edge cross-check of all 19 main-plan edges and the branch gate edges); arrow direction is dependency → dependent; critical path length 5 is within the M-with-new-component heuristic (4-5); E002/E004/E005 correctly parallel (no unjustified serialization); no konflux-onboarding epic exists and none is needed (architecture context confirms Dockerfile.konflux and konflux-central synced pipelines for models-as-a-service; the build-pipeline AC on E002 follows Rule 25). All three branch files set `branch`, `gated_by`, and `gate_failure_impact` correctly, and no main-plan epic lists a branch epic in its `dependencies`.\n68\t\n69\tHowever, there is a missing artifact dependency discoverable from the branch epics' own content. BRANCH-A-E012 and BRANCH-B-E012 each have the acceptance criterion \"A MaaSSubscription with token rate limits configured returns HTTP 429 when the quota is exceeded (TokenRateLimitPolicy enforcement functional on CKS)\", and BRANCH-C-E012's enforcement AC likewise requires the MaaS request path on the cluster. Per the architecture context, the TokenRateLimitPolicy is created by the MaaSSubscriptionReconciler in maas-controller, and the MaaS CRD family/controller on xKS is installed by E002's Helm charts (E002 scope: \"CRDs and validating admission webhooks installed via the chart\"). The branch epics list only E001 in `dependencies` — there is no edge from E002, so if a branch activates it can be scheduled before the charts exist and its own AC is unsatisfiable. Branch-to-main-plan edges are permitted (only main-plan → branch edges are forbidden), so this edge is representable and required. Related observation (not separately penalized, since the review rules sanction the pattern): E007's scope (\"with RHCL/Kuadrant deployed per the selected deployment mechanism\") consumes the selected branch's deployment; this coupling is only implied through the shared E001 gate, and the 429 validation AC is duplicated between E007 and the branch epics (see Criterion 3).\n70\t\n71\t### 3. Epic Boundaries (2/2)\n72\tComponent/team tuples are consistent and separate where they differ: E001 and the branch A epic (Inference Gateway, RHCL / OpenShift), E002-E005/E007-E010 (Model as a Service, MaaS), E006 (Monitoring, xKS Platform), E011 (Documentation, MaaS), branch B (xKS Platform), branch C (MaaS). No epic bundles multiple components or teams inappropriately; no epic is clearly multi-sprint. No work-product type mixing: the docs-authoring work is isolated in E011; the known-limitations document in E007 is a validation-findings deliverable mandated by the strategy's own AC, not content authoring. Two minor boundary observations: (a) E001 bundles the CKS Prometheus/Loki accessibility confirmation, which the strategy's open-questions table assigns to xKS Platform / CoreWeave, into an RHCL / OpenShift-team epic (acknowledged in the epic's notes and dual-covered by E006/E009 ACs, so not incorrect execution, just cross-team scope in one epic); (b) the HTTP 429 rate-limiting validation AC appears in both E007 and BRANCH-A/B-E012 — redundant validation scope that blurs the boundary between the deployment-mechanism branch and the CKS validation epic (neither rebuilds the other's work, so it falls below the duplication threshold). Two minors, no score reduction.\n73\t\n74\t### 4. Type Correctness (2/2)\n75\tE001 is a genuine Investigation: its outcome (which of three bounded deployment options) determines the shape of downstream work, and three conditional branches are attached to it. All Implementation epics produce concrete artifacts (charts, code, deployments, dashboards, documents, validation findings). Gating metadata is correct: E003 and E007 both list E001 in `dependencies`, set `gated_by: E001`, and populate `gate_failure_impact` with `action` and `fallback_approach` (rewrite under the Envoy ext_authz contingency — a true scope-changing gate, not a scheduling dependency). No epic lists an Investigation in `dependencies` without `gated_by`. The branch epics' `gated_by` targets appear in their direct `dependencies`. E006/E008/E009/E010 are described as outcome-gated in the summary's Tier 2, which is consistent with their transitive gating through E007 (E001 → E007 → E006/E008/E009/E010); no direct gate metadata is required for them.\n76\t\n77\t### 5. AI Implementability Scoring (1/2)\n78\tSignal-set usage is correct: E001 carries `investigation_signals` (5-signal rubric) with `ai_signals: null`; all Implementation epics including E011 and the branch epics carry `ai_signals`. Frontmatter values match the rationale tables in every ai-signals file. E001's investigation signals are well-scored per the rubric (source_accessibility 0 for a decision still under review matches the rubric's example exactly; cluster_hardware_dependence -2 and human_judgment_required -2 for a gating cluster-validation/org-commitment question). E006, E008, E010, E011, and the branch signal sets are conservative and justified. Two arguable values with inaccurate rationales: (a) E003's `architecture_claims: 1` rationale claims the strategy's citations (auth-policy.yaml, Tenant CRD OIDC fields) are \"corroborated by the models-as-a-service architecture context file\" — the context file documents the AuthPolicy's optional OIDC JWT validation and the `/internal/v1/api-keys/validate` callback, but contains no `oidcIssuerURL`/`oidcClientID`/`oidcAudience` Tenant CRD fields and no auth-policy.yaml path; (b) E004's `pattern_precedent: 1` and `architecture_claims: 1` rationales claim architecture-context corroboration for the \"CEL predicates over `auth.identity.groups` in maassubscription_controller.go\" mechanism — the context file documents the MaaSSubscriptionReconciler and its TokenRateLimitPolicy creation but contains no CEL/group-claim mechanism and no maassubscription_controller.go reference. The strategy asserts these mechanisms, so the values are arguable rather than contradicted, but the rationales overstate corroboration (architecture_claims plausibly 0 for E003/E004).\n79\t\n80\t### 6. Acceptance Criteria Quality (2/2)\n81\tEvery epic has testable Given/When/Then or pass/fail ACs derived from the strategy's acceptance criteria. Rule-mandated ACs are present: E002 has the build-pipeline-green AC (Rule 25), E011 has the technical-review sign-off AC, and the replacement-flavored epics (E003, E004, E005, BRANCH-C) each carry an explicit additive/backwards-compatibility AC. No non-testable schedule-constraint ACs were found (the MaaS 3.4 build-timing risk is correctly carried in epic notes/health warnings, not ACs). No epic body references sibling epics by draft ID (verified by grep of all 14 epic bodies). One minor: E002's scope includes \"Composability with the wider RHAII xKS installation tooling\" but no AC tests it, while the summary's health warning 8 states it is \"captured as an AC on the Helm packaging epic\" — the summary claims an AC that does not exist in the epic (summary/epic mismatch; the scope item itself is present).\n82\t\n83\t### 7. Completeness (2/2)\n84\tStrategy scope is fully accounted for: user management strategy and identity flow (E003, E011), CKS/AKS validation with documented engineering delta and known limitations (E007, E008), rate limiting (E007 + branches), tier/subscription management (E004), API access control (E003/E007), observability metrics/logging/tracing (E005/E006, E009, E010), Helm-only packaging with no OLM (E002), and the operator-integration exclusion is respected. All four failure-mode ACs from the strategy are allocated (OIDC outage → E003; Limitador termination, cert rotation, PostgreSQL unavailability → E007, with cert renewal also on E002). All three bounded outcomes of the strategic dependency have branch epics. Strategy-level context is carried forward (schedule risk, empty Staff Engineer Input section, AKS/CKS contradiction, CRD count discrepancy all surfaced as health warnings; open questions resolved within the owning epics). A docs-authoring epic exists (E011) despite Documentation Support being listed as \"Pending human review\" rather than \"Yes\". One minor: the strategy's PostgreSQL-unavailability mitigation (in-memory LRU cache, TTL 5 min, for validated API key hashes) is not explicitly assigned to any epic's scope — E003 owns the OIDC-outage caching question but not the API-key-hash cache, and E007's AC defers to \"the documented caching behavior\" without an owner for that behavior. Core scope is complete.\n85\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-88fdf3c72f5593de","agent_id":"a2a63aa4e281263c5","session_id":"03f22d79-03f4-4cd2-8583-e4fd3448986e","uuid":"34ff7c83-d627-408c-babe-69762267ea84","timestamp":"2026-10-22T05:20:26.064Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-9e08b53e7cc80e43","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_des