{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9d308ad49db8a806","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8003\n3\tepic_count: 11\n4\tcritical_path_length: 5\n5\tdecompose_complete: true\n6\ttriage: null\n7\ttriage_rationale: null\n8\t---\n9\t# RHAISTRAT-8003 Decomposition — RHAII MaaS on xKS\n10\t\n11\tStrategy: RHAISTRAT-8003 (RHAII MaaS on xKS), priority Blocker, Dev Preview scope targeting Summit (May 2026).\n12\t\n13\t**Triage:** Not triaged as below-threshold or docs-only. The strategy is M-sized (3-5 sprints), spans multiple components (maas-api, maas-controller, Kuadrant/RHCL, Gateway API, observability) and three teams (MaaS, xKS Platform, RHCL / OpenShift), and contains genuine unknowns (RHCL official xKS support) whose answers change which downstream epics exist and what they do. Full decomposition follows.\n14\t\n15\t**Staff Engineer Input:** The Staff Engineer Input section of the strategy is empty (template placeholder comments only) — no overrides to log. The technical approach repeatedly quotes \"per Staff Engineer Input\" guidance (CKS as priority platform; observability as core value proposition); that inline guidance is treated as strategy content. See Health Warnings.\n16\t\n17\t## Epic List (main plan)\n18\t\n19\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n20\t| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n21\t| RHAISTRAT-8003-E001 | Confirm RHCL/Kuadrant xKS support and validate the deployment mechanism on CKS | Investigation | RHCL / OpenShift | P0 |\n22\t| RHAISTRAT-8003-E002 | MaaS Helm chart packaging for xKS deployment | Implementation | MaaS | P0 |\n23\t| RHAISTRAT-8003-E003 | External OIDC authentication integration for MaaS on xKS | Implementation | MaaS | P0 |\n24\t| RHAISTRAT-8003-E004 | Configurable OIDC group claim path mapping for subscription tiers | Implementation | MaaS | P0 |\n25\t| RHAISTRAT-8003-E005 | Prometheus metrics instrumentation in maas-api | Implementation | MaaS | P0 |\n26\t| RHAISTRAT-8003-E006 | Perses dashboards and CKS Prometheus integration for MaaS observability | Implementation | xKS Platform | P0 |\n27\t| RHAISTRAT-8003-E007 | MaaS deployment validation on CKS | Implementation | MaaS | P0 |\n28\t| RHAISTRAT-8003-E008 | MaaS deployment validation on AKS | Implementation | MaaS | P0 |\n29\t| RHAISTRAT-8003-E009 | Structured logging for MaaS request-path events with CKS Loki integration | Implementation | MaaS | P1 |\n30\t| RHAISTRAT-8003-E010 | Distributed tracing across the MaaS request path on xKS | Implementation | MaaS | P2 |\n31\t| RHAISTRAT-8003-E011 | User identity flow documentation for MaaS on xKS | Implementation (docs-authoring) | MaaS | P0 |\n32\t\n33\tMain-plan epics: 11 (1 Investigation, 10 Implementation). Three conditional branch epics (RHAISTRAT-8003-BRANCH-A/B/C-E012) are additionally attached to the gating Investigation as plan documents (see Conditional Branches).\n34\t\n35\t## Conditional Branches\n36\t\n37\tThe strategy's strategic dependency — RHCL official xKS support for Kuadrant — is an unknown with exactly three bounded outcomes, enumerated in the strategy as deployment options in priority order. Per the conditional decomposition rules, the Kuadrant deployment mechanism is expressed as branch epics gated by the Investigation (RHAISTRAT-8003-E001):\n38\t\n39\t| Branch | Outcome | Epic | Team | gate_failure_impact |\n40\t| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n41\t| A | RHCL officially supports xKS and provides Helm charts | RHAISTRAT-8003-BRANCH-A-E012 — Deploy Kuadrant on xKS via official RHCL Helm charts | RHCL / OpenShift | remove (fall back to branch B) |\n42\t| B | RHCL support not committed; standalone deployment viable | RHAISTRAT-8003-BRANCH-B-E012 — Deploy standalone Authorino + Limitador via Helm on xKS | xKS Platform | remove (fall back to branch C) |\n43\t| C | Neither Kuadrant path viable (contingency) | RHAISTRAT-8003-BRANCH-C-E012 — Direct Envoy ext_authz integration for MaaS auth and rate limiting | MaaS | rewrite (escalate to product leadership; reassess Dev Preview scope) |\n44\t\n45\tBranch epics are not standalone Jira issues; no main-plan epic depends on a branch epic. Main-plan epics that depend on the *outcome* (E003, E007) depend on the Investigation itself with `gated_by`.\n46\t\n47\t## Dependency DAG\n48\t\n49\tMain plan (arrows point from dependency to dependent):\n50\t\n51\t```mermaid\n52\tgraph TD\n53\t    E001[E001 Investigation: RHCL/Kuadrant xKS support] --> E003[E003 OIDC auth integration]\n54\t    E001 --> E007[E007 CKS deployment validation]\n55\t    E002[E002 Helm chart packaging] --> E007\n56\t    E003 --> E007\n57\t    E004[E004 Group claim path mapping] --> E007\n58\t    E005[E005 Metrics instrumentation] --> E006[E006 Perses dashboards + CKS Prometheus]\n59\t    E007 --> E006\n60\t    E007 --> E008[E008 AKS deployment validation]\n61\t    E007 --> E009[E009 Structured logging + Loki]\n62\t    E007 --> E010[E010 Distributed tracing]\n63\t    E002 --> E011[E011 Identity flow docs]\n64\t    E003 --> E011\n65\t    E004 --> E011\n66\t    E005 --> E011\n67\t    E006 --> E011\n68\t    E007 --> E011\n69\t    E008 --> E011\n70\t    E009 --> E011\n71\t    E010 --> E011\n72\t```\n73\t\n74\tBranch epics (conditional, excluded from the main-plan critical path):\n75\t\n76\t```mermaid\n77\tgraph TD\n78\t    E001[E001 Investigation] -.-> BA[BRANCH-A-E012 RHCL Helm charts]\n79\t    E001 -.-> BB[BRANCH-B-E012 Standalone Authorino + Limitador]\n80\t    E001 -.-> BC[BRANCH-C-E012 Envoy ext_authz contingency]\n81\t```\n82\t\n83\t## DAG Justification\n84\t\n85\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n86\t| --- | --- | --- |\n87\t| E001 → E003 | Rule 3 (Investigation gates downstream) | The auth-enforcement approach (Authorino AuthPolicy vs. the Envoy ext_authz contingency) is determined by the investigation outcome. True gate: `rewrite` if the contingency is selected. |\n88\t| E001 → E007 | Rule 3 | CKS deployment validation requires the Kuadrant deployment mechanism to be selected and validated. True gate: `rewrite` (scope changes under the contingency). |\n89\t| E002 → E007 | Rule 13 (artifact consumer) | CKS validation deploys MaaS via the Helm charts the packaging epic produces. |\n90\t| E003 → E007 | Rule 13 | CKS validation exercises the external OIDC authentication the auth epic produces. |\n91\t| E004 → E007 | Rule 13 | Tier-based rate limiting validation on CKS requires the configurable group claim path. |\n92\t| E005 → E006 | Rule 13 (artifact consumer) | Dashboards and Prometheus integration consume the metrics the instrumentation epic exposes. |\n93\t| E007 → E006 | Rule 13 | The observability integration ACs are measured against a deployed MaaS instance on CKS with live data. |\n94\t| E007 → E008 | Rules 12/13 + strategy sequencing | Strategy explicitly sequences CKS first (priority platform per quoted staff guidance); AKS validation builds on the charts fixed and the engineering delta documented during CKS validation. |\n95\t| E007 → E009 | Rule 13 | Logging ACs are measured by querying CKS Grafana Loki against a deployed instance. |\n96\t| E007 → E010 | Rule 13 | End-to-end trace validation requires the full request path deployed on xKS. |\n97\t| E002…E010 → E011 | Rule 11 (docs last) | The docs-authoring epic is blocked by every main-plan Implementation epic; docs describe what was built. Edges do not trigger priority inheritance (docs-authoring priority exception). |\n98\t| (none) konflux-onboarding edges | Rule 10 | No konflux-onboarding epic exists: the models-as-a-service repository is already Konflux-onboarded (architecture context shows Dockerfile.konflux and konflux-central synced pipelines). The Helm chart build pipeline is folded into E002 as a \"build pipeline green\" AC (Rule 25), never as a DAG edge. |\n99\t| E002 ∥ E003 ∥ E004 ∥ E005 (no edges) | Rule 15 | Same repository, different areas (packaging vs. API auth vs. controller CRD/predicates vs. metrics instrumentation). Coupling is coordination risk, not dependency. |\n100\t| E001 ∥ E002/E004/E005 (no edges) | Rule 17 / Step 5.5 | Tier 1 independent work: packaging, group-claim mapping, and metrics instrumentation deliver partial value regardless of the RHCL outcome. |\n101\t\n102\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n103\t\n104\t| HLR | Priority | Requirement (abbreviated) | Epic(s) |\n105\t| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n106\t| HLR-1 | P0 | MaaS successfully deployed on CKS | E007 |\n107\t| HLR-2 | P0 | MaaS successfully deployed on AKS | E008 |\n108\t| HLR-3 | P0 | Token rate limiting functional on xKS | E007, BRANCH-A/B/C-E012 |\n109\t| HLR-4 | P0 | External OIDC authentication validated | E003 (end-to-end on E007) |\n110\t| HLR-5 | P0 | API key authentication (sk-oai-*) functional on xKS | E003 (end-to-end on E007) |\n111\t| HLR-6 | P0 | Configurable OIDC group claim path mapping | E004 |\n112\t| HLR-7 | P0 | RHCL official xKS support confirmed and deployment mechanism validated | E001, BRANCH-A/B/C-E012 |\n113\t| HLR-8 | P0 | Observability metrics functional (token usage, rate limiting, API key lifecycle) | E005, E006 |\n114\t| HLR-9 | P1 | Tier/subscription management functional with OIDC group claims | E004 (same surface as HLR-6 — bundled, see below) |\n115\t| HLR-10 | P1 | Observability logging functional (auth events, rate limit enforcement, subscription selection) | E009, BRANCH-A/B-E012 (Kuadrant-side logging config), BRANCH-C-E012 (Envoy-native enforcement-path logging — no Kuadrant under this branch) |\n116\t| HLR-11 | P1 | Known limitations documented | E007 (consolidated document), E008 (AKS-specific findings) |\n117\t| HLR-12 | P2 | User identity flow documented for xKS deployments | E011 |\n118\t| HLR-13 | P2 | Distributed tracing functional (gateway → … → backend) | E010 |\n119\t\n120\tAll 13 HLRs covered; no orphaned requirements.\n121\t\n122\t**HLR consolidation note (HLR-6 + HLR-9):** \"Configurable OIDC group claim path mapping\" (P0) and \"Tier/subscription management functional with OIDC group claims\" (P1) describe the same controller surface (MaaSSubscription tier mapping via CEL predicates over group claims) — the P1 HLR is the functional expression of the P0 capability. They are one capability covered by a single epic (E004) at the higher priority (P0); splitting would build the same surface twice with an artificial serialization edge.\n123\t\n124\t## Component Mappings (canonical component log)\n125\t\n126\tEvery epic's `component` is copied verbatim from `.context/rhai-components.txt`. Non-obvious mappings:\n127\t\n128\t| Strategy component | Canonical component | Epics | Note |\n129\t| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n130\t| models-as-a-service (maas-api), models-as-a-service (maas-controller), payload-processing BBR ext_proc | Model as a Service | E002–E005, E007–E010 | Single canonical name for the whole models-as-a-service repository span. |\n131\t| Kuadrant (Authorino), Kuadrant (Limitador) | Inference Gateway | E001, BRANCH-A/B/C-E012 | No canonical \"Kuadrant\" component exists; Kuadrant is the gateway-plane auth/rate-limiting stack behind the inference gateway, so the closest canonical name is Inference Gateway. |\n132\t| Gateway API | (no separate epic) | — | Passive/config-only: the Gateway API implementation on CKS (Istio) is confirmed available per the strategy dependency table. GatewayClass configurability is folded into the Helm chart packaging epic (E002); functioning gateway is an acceptance criterion on the CKS validation epic (E007). No code changes in the Gateway API component itself. |\n133\t| Observability (Prometheus, Perses) | Monitoring | E006 | Instrumentation (E005) stays under Model as a Service; the xKS Platform integration epic maps to Monitoring. |\n134\t| Documentation (user identity flow) | Documentation | E011 | docs-authoring work. |\n135\t\n136\t**New-component handling:** models-as-a-service is in the platform (architecture context: models-as-a-service.md) — no onboarding chain. Kuadrant is NOT in the architecture context and is external RHCL product consumed via Helm (not midstream-forked), so Rule 22 applies rather than Rule 20: a validation Investigation (E001) plus provisioning Implementations (the branch epics). No repo-onboarding/license-validation onboarding chain is generated for it; license validation of standalone components is an AC on BRANCH-B-E012.\n137\t\n138\t## Health Warnings\n139\t\n140\t1. **AKS/CKS scope contradiction:** the strategy's \"What Gets Delivered\" section says \"validated MaaS deployment on at least one xKS platform (AKS or CKS)\", while the acceptance criteria and HLRs require both (\"AKS AND CKS\"). Decomposed for both platforms (separate P0 epics) per the HLRs; PM should confirm.\n141\t2. **Tracing priority inconsistency:** the NFR section labels distributed tracing \"(P1)\" while the HLR lists it as P2. The HLR list is used (P2, E010).\n142\t3. **Docs epic priority derivation:** the docs-authoring epic (E011) derives its priority from the strategy frontmatter `priority: Blocker` per the docs-authoring priority exception. Blocker sits above Critical in severity, so it maps to the P0 bucket — even though the underlying HLR-12 is P2. The docs priority does not propagate upstream (edges E002…E010 → E011 do not trigger priority inheritance).\n143\t4. **MaaS 3.4 GA build timing (schedule risk, not an AC):** stable build availability is unconfirmed (open question owned by MaaS PM). Mitigations per strategy: RC build, parallel CKS validation, RHOAI 3.3 fallback demo. This is carried in epic risks, not as acceptance criteria.\n144\t5. **Empty Staff Engineer Input section:** the strategy body repeatedly cites \"per Staff Engineer Input\" (CKS priority, observability as core value proposition), but the actual Staff Engineer Input section contains only template placeholders. The inline quotes were treated as strategy content; recommend verifying with the staff engineer before implementation.\n145\t6. **Pending process gates:** the strategy's prerequisites list build onboarding, license validation, and documentation support as \"Pending human review\" without detail. The Helm chart build pipeline is folded into E002 as an AC; license validation of standalone Kuadrant components is an AC on BRANCH-B-E012; documentation support is realized as the docs-authoring epic (E011).\n146\t7. **CRD count discrepancy:** the strategy states maas-controller manages 5 CRDs; the architecture context lists 8 (including AITenant, Config, MaasTenantConfig). No decomposition impact; noted for accuracy.\n147\t8. **xKS Platform installer integration:** the Impacted Teams table mentions \"Helm chart integration with RHAII xKS deployment strategy\" for xKS Platform, but no HLR covers it. It is captured as an AC on the Helm packaging epic (chart composable with the RHAII xKS installation) rather than a separate epic.\n148\t\n149\t## Tiered Delivery\n150\t\n151\tThe RHCL official xKS support dependency has uncertain timing (external, unknown status). Partial value is deliverable without it, so the work is tiered:\n152\t\n153\t- **Tier 1 (independent, start immediately):** E001 (risk-reduction investigation), E002 (Helm packaging), E004 (group claim path mapping), E005 (metrics instrumentation). These deliver value and reduce risk regardless of the RHCL outcome.\n154\t- **Tier 2 (gated by the RHCL outcome):** E003 (approach-gated: auth enforcement mechanism), E006–E008 (deployment validation and observability integration), E009–E010 (logging/tracing validated on deployed instances), and the selected branch deployment epic (BRANCH-A/B/C-E012).\n155\t\n156\tIf the RHCL dependency resolves unfavorably (branch C contingency), Tier 1 work remains valid and the Tier 2 authentication approach is rewritten per the gate failure impact.\n157\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-88fdf3c72f5593de","agent_id":"a2a63aa4e281263c5","session_id":"03f22d79-03f4-4cd2-8583-e4fd3448986e","uuid":"5f111f69-c5df-4d68-95f1-8089c514706d","timestamp":"2026-10-22T05:20:4