{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-80d90b0fa418626b","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tepic_id: RHAISTRAT-8003-BRANCH-C-E012\n3\ttitle: Direct Envoy ext_authz integration for MaaS auth and rate limiting\n4\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8003\n5\tcomponent: Inference Gateway\n6\tteam: MaaS\n7\ttype: Implementation\n8\tpriority: P0\n9\tbranch: C\n10\tgated_by: RHAISTRAT-8003-E001\n11\tdependencies:\n12\t- RHAISTRAT-8003-E001\n13\tgate_failure_impact:\n14\t  action: rewrite\n15\t  fallback_approach: If the contingency also fails, escalate to product leadership\n16\t    and reassess the MaaS xKS Dev Preview scope per the strategy's risk mitigation\n17\tai_signals:\n18\t  change_specificity: 0\n19\t  pattern_precedent: 0\n20\t  adapter_pattern: 0\n21\t  existing_foundation: 0\n22\t  open_questions: -1\n23\t  external_dependency: 0\n24\t  human_process_gates: 0\n25\t  repo_access: 1\n26\t  architecture_claims: 0\n27\timplementation_type: null\n28\tinvestigation_signals: null\n29\tai_implementability: Medium\n30\tai_implementability_score: 0\n31\tjira_key: null\n32\t---\n33\t# Direct Envoy ext_authz integration for MaaS auth and rate limiting (Branch C)\n34\t\n35\t## Description\n36\t\n37\tBranch C of the RHCL/Kuadrant deployment mechanism (contingency): neither the official RHCL Helm charts nor standalone Kuadrant deployment is viable, so MaaS authentication and rate-limiting enforcement are integrated directly with Envoy ext_authz at the gateway, bypassing Kuadrant (Authorino + Limitador). This reworks the MaaS-side enforcement integration while preserving the external OIDC validation and API key flows. This branch epic applies only if the RHCL/Kuadrant xKS support investigation selects outcome C.\n38\t\n39\t## Scope\n40\t\n41\t- Design and implement Envoy-native authentication enforcement via ext_authz covering the OIDC JWT validation path and the API key validation path (the maas-api callback validation flow).\n42\t- Define the rate-limiting enforcement approach without Limitador's TokenRateLimitPolicy, preserving per-subscription token rate limits with HTTP 429 on exceeded quota (or a documented equivalent).\n43\t- Preserve the strategy's identity model: customer-provided OIDC provider, API keys OIDC-independent.\n44\t- Structured logging for the Envoy-native enforcement path: authentication decisions and rate-limit enforcement events emitted by the ext_authz integration, using the same structured JSON schema as the maas-api-side logging (timestamp, user identity, authentication method, decision, reason when denied) and integrated with CKS Grafana Loki. Under the Kuadrant deployment paths this logging is carried by the Kuadrant components' logging configuration; under this contingency there is no Kuadrant, so the gateway-side enforcement logging is MaaS-owned here.\n45\t- Keep changes additive/configuration-driven so RHOAI MaaS GA behavior is unchanged.\n46\t\n47\t## Acceptance Criteria\n48\t\n49\t- A design for Envoy-native enforcement exists covering OIDC JWT validation and API key validation at the gateway, reviewed and recorded.\n50\t- Enforcement is integrated with the gateway on a CKS cluster with equivalent behavior to the Kuadrant path: valid OIDC tokens and API keys accepted; invalid credentials rejected; exceeded token quotas return HTTP 429 (or the documented equivalent with rationale).\n51\t- RHOAI MaaS GA behavior is unchanged (additive/configuration-driven change only).\n52\t- Gateway-side enforcement decisions (authentication allow/deny with reason, rate-limit enforcement with exceeded quota) are emitted as structured JSON log entries retrievable from CKS Grafana Loki with label filters, matching the schema of the maas-api-side structured logging.\n53\t\n54\t## HLR Traceability\n55\t\n56\t- HLR-7 (P0): RHCL official xKS support status confirmed and deployment mechanism validated (this branch realizes the contingency mechanism).\n57\t- HLR-3 (P0): Token rate limiting functional on xKS (Envoy-native enforcement path).\n58\t- HLR-4 (P0) / HLR-5 (P0): external OIDC and API key authentication remain functional under the reworked enforcement (the primary OIDC integration epic is rewritten accordingly on this branch).\n59\t- HLR-10 (P1): Observability logging functional — the gateway-side enforcement-logging half (under the Kuadrant paths this is carried by the deployment branches; under this contingency it is carried here since no Kuadrant exists).\n60\t\n61\t## Notes\n62\t\n63\t- This is a conditional branch epic (branch C), gated by the RHCL/Kuadrant xKS support investigation; it is attached to that investigation as a plan document and is not a standalone Jira issue.\n64\t- Gate failure impact: if the contingency also fails, the work is rewritten — escalate to product leadership and reassess the MaaS xKS Dev Preview scope, per the strategy's risk mitigation (\"lack of official RHCL support undermines the long-term viability of MaaS on xKS\").\n65\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-88fdf3c72f5593de","agent_id":"a2a63aa4e281263c5","session_id":"03f22d79-03f4-4cd2-8583-e4fd3448986e","uuid":"97587a1e-cba8-44f2-9f98-682e61c74efa","timestamp":"2026-10-22T05:21:00.508Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-80d90b0fa418626b","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Re-revise RHAISTRAT-8003 decomposition"}
