{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-afc39d81056ea1a2","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tepic_id: RHAISTRAT-8003-E001\n3\ttitle: Confirm RHCL/Kuadrant xKS support and validate the deployment mechanism on\n4\t  CKS\n5\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8003\n6\tcomponent: Inference Gateway\n7\tteam: RHCL / OpenShift\n8\ttype: Investigation\n9\tpriority: P0\n10\tdependencies: []\n11\tinvestigation_signals:\n12\t  question_specificity: 1\n13\t  source_accessibility: 0\n14\t  local_runnability: 0\n15\t  cluster_hardware_dependence: -2\n16\t  human_judgment_required: -2\n17\timplementation_type: null\n18\tai_signals: null\n19\tai_implementability: Low\n20\tai_implementability_score: -3\n21\tjira_key: null\n22\tbranch: null\n23\tgated_by: null\n24\tgate_failure_impact: null\n25\t---\n26\t# Confirm RHCL/Kuadrant xKS support and validate the deployment mechanism on CKS\n27\t\n28\t## Description\n29\t\n30\tResolves the strategy's strategic dependency on RHCL (Red Hat Common Libraries / Kuadrant) for MaaS authentication and rate limiting on non-OpenShift Kubernetes. MaaS on RHOAI relies on RHCL deploying Kuadrant (Authorino + Limitador) via OLM; on xKS (Helm-only, no OLM), the official RHCL xKS support status is unknown. This investigation confirms whether RHCL officially supports xKS deployment, validates that Kuadrant installs and functions on CKS (the priority platform) with the Istio GatewayClass, and selects the deployment mechanism that all downstream MaaS xKS deployment and authentication work builds on.\n31\t\n32\tThe strategy enumerates exactly three bounded deployment options, in priority order: (1) official RHCL Helm charts, (2) standalone Authorino + Limitador via Helm, (3) direct Envoy ext_authz integration as contingency. The selected outcome determines the shape of the downstream deployment work; conditional branch plans are attached to this epic for each outcome.\n33\t\n34\t## Scope\n35\t\n36\t- Engage the OpenShift RHCL team to confirm the official xKS support commitment and timeline; escalate to product leadership if no commitment is obtained (per the strategy's Sprint 1 mitigation).\n37\t- Confirm whether official RHCL/Kuadrant Helm charts for xKS (CKS in particular) exist or are planned.\n38\t- Validate that Kuadrant (Authorino + Limitador) installs and runs on a CKS cluster with the Istio GatewayClass.\n39\t- Select and document the deployment mechanism among the strategy's three bounded options, with rationale and findings (what works, what breaks, engineering delta per option).\n40\t- Confirm access requirements for the CKS Prometheus and Grafana Loki infrastructure that observability work assumes (bundled here as the same Sprint-1 validation pass over CKS infrastructure assumptions; owned per the strategy's open-questions table by xKS Platform / CoreWeave, so this item is executed in coordination with that team — see Notes).\n41\t\n42\t## Acceptance Criteria\n43\t\n44\t- A findings document states whether RHCL officially supports xKS deployment, including the commitment and timeline obtained from the OpenShift RHCL team, or an escalation record if no commitment was obtained.\n45\t- Kuadrant (Authorino + Limitador) has been installed on a CKS cluster with the Istio GatewayClass and reports healthy, OR a documented failure analysis shows why it cannot be installed and which of the strategy's fallback options applies.\n46\t- A deployment mechanism is selected from the strategy's three options with recorded rationale, sufficient for the downstream deployment, authentication, and validation work to proceed without further unknowns on this question.\n47\t- Availability (or unavailability) of official RHCL/Kuadrant Helm charts for xKS is confirmed and recorded.\n48\t- The CKS Prometheus scrape-target and Grafana Loki log-aggregation accessibility assumed by the observability requirements is confirmed or refuted, with findings recorded.\n49\t\n50\t## HLR Traceability\n51\t\n52\t- HLR-7 (P0): RHCL official xKS support status confirmed and deployment mechanism validated.\n53\t- De-risks HLR-3 (token rate limiting functional on xKS) by validating the Limitador enforcement path on CKS.\n54\t\n55\t## Notes\n56\t\n57\t- Bounded outcomes (3) → conditional decomposition branches are attached to this epic as plan documents; downstream epics depending on the outcome reference this investigation, not a specific branch.\n58\t- The CKS Prometheus/Loki accessibility confirmation is assigned by the strategy's open-questions table to xKS Platform / CoreWeave, not to this epic's RHCL / OpenShift team. It is bundled into this epic's Sprint-1 validation pass because it requires the same CKS cluster access, and it is dual-covered: the Perses dashboards / CKS Prometheus integration epic and the structured-logging / Loki integration epic each re-confirm accessibility as their own acceptance criteria, with findings shared across all three.\n59\t- Schedule risk carried from the strategy: this is a Sprint 1 validation; delay here compresses the Summit (May 2026) timeline.\n60\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b2b0805231d30147","agent_id":"ab39e52713d563351","session_id":"03f22d79-03f4-4cd2-8583-e4fd3448986e","uuid":"b6df8b1f-ebd3-4fdc-adc5-cd2996539fed","timestamp":"2026-10-22T05:27:00.916Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-afc39d81056ea1a2","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Final review RHAISTRAT-8003"}
